WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:05.620
Native dependencies bring ABI and runtime concerns that managed environments may hide.

2
00:00:05.720 --> 00:00:17.240
A library compiled with different compiler settings, runtime libraries, standard library versions, structure packing, exception behavior, threading assumptions, or allocator expectations can behave unpredictably.

3
00:00:17.340 --> 00:00:24.140
The application may compile cleanly and still fail in subtle ways when the wrong binary combination is deployed.

4
00:00:24.420 --> 00:00:26.660
Dynamic libraries add another layer.

5
00:00:26.760 --> 00:00:33.080
The application should know which library is loaded in production, where it comes from, and how runtime search paths are controlled.

6
00:00:33.180 --> 00:00:41.260
Development machines, CI agents, containers, installers, and customer systems may have different library locations.

7
00:00:41.360 --> 00:00:47.440
If that difference is not tested, the release artifact may depend on something the team did not intend to ship.

8
00:00:47.720 --> 00:00:51.160
Platform compatibility should be documented rather than assumed.

9
00:00:51.260 --> 00:01:04.060
Windows, Linux, macOS, embedded targets, and containerized environments differ in loader behavior, packaging conventions, C++ runtime choices, path handling, and available system packages.

10
00:01:04.160 --> 00:01:12.320
Cross-platform products should make these assumptions explicit so reviewers can identify when a dependency change affects one platform differently from another.

11
00:01:12.600 --> 00:01:15.400
Testing should include real deployment artifacts.

12
00:01:15.500 --> 00:01:22.380
It is not enough to test the development build if the packaged product uses a different library layout or runtime search path.

13
00:01:22.480 --> 00:01:33.200
Integration testing, installer validation, package inspection, and startup diagnostics can all help confirm that the application loads the intended dependency versions with compatible settings.
